From 9a1f1d6839d6f261d1e69b4e3cd93c8bfd69ed28 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Victor C." <34163765+vicajilau@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 08:16:12 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] js: Say which workspace index each caller wants

meta_workspace_index() is gone in favour of two explicit accessors, since
with per-monitor workspaces its answer depended on a setting the caller did
not look at.

Most of these want the global one, because the number does not stay in the
shell: app.open_new_window() and shellWorkspaceLaunch() hand it to mutter as
a startup sequence hint, which is resolved against every workspace that
exists, and Main.activateWindow() compares it with
get_active_workspace_index(). Those were passing a monitor-relative
position, so dropping an app on a workspace of any monitor but the first
opened it on the wrong workspace.

The ones that stay monitor-relative are the ones showing a monitor to the
user: the workspace switcher OSD, the workspaces view of one monitor, and
the thumbnail ordering within it.

Two comparisons between a workspace and the active one of a monitor used
the monitor-relative position, which two workspaces on different monitors
can share. They compare global positions now, which are unique.

The switcher OSD also had a dead branch reading a monitor_index property
that does not exist, so it fell through to the ambiguous index and happened
to show the right number. It now asks for the monitor-relative one.
